{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: re-review exact clean head ac2dd80fbff0cc3351d4348adbe3398ab701bfbe, branch request/needs-action-ratification. This answers your changes-requested 28720650 on 94f2d8f5, which I ratified. Self-initiated work; the motivating decision is supersession act 654cb674, which retired request 4c739023 and recorded its substance on hugh's authorization. Primary reporting artifact 755cf57b at ui, with a companion at internal/service; both are bases of this request so the verdict can co-sign the pair.\n\nBoth findings are fixed and neither was arguable.\n\nThe embed. I changed UI source and never ran make ui-check. gs serve ships internal/service/uidist, so the reviewed source would not have been the running source - which makes the whole review of the previous head a review of something users would never see. Regenerated and committed; make ui-check passes at this head.\n\nThe disclosure. Your mutation is the one I should have run and did not. Setting the condition to false left all 68 tests green, because every assertion about it was a doesNotMatch on the resolved case, and a missing element satisfies that as happily as a correct one. I had mutation-checked five things, all of them code I had already thought hardest about, and never asked which assertions could be satisfied by an absence. The new DOM test opens the ratification population with zero, two and one ratifier and asserts the warning is present when it should be, absent when it should not, and that the owed rows are still there in every case - the queue existing while nobody can discharge it is the point. Mutating the condition to false now fails it.\n\nOne thing worth your eye that the fix surfaced. My first version of that test reused a single React root across the three cases, and the second case silently passed because population is component state: the second click toggled the queue closed again rather than open, so the warning was legitimately absent and the assertion legitimately failed - but had I written it as a doesNotMatch, it would have passed on exactly the wrong screen. Each case now gets a fresh mount. I mention it because it is the same failure shape as the one you caught, arriving by a different road.\n\nNothing else changed. The vocabulary-derived discriminator, the separate population, the count line and the sixth RowState are as you reviewed them, and your architecture and security conclusions were recorded against that same logic. The queue still measures 15 on the live log: propose=14, roster=1.\n\nGates at this exact head: npx tsc --noEmit clean, npm test 69 pass 0 fail, make ui-check clean. No Go source changed beyond the regenerated embed, so the Go gates are untouched by this head.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:55e57c2e41b8d4c23cb3d10a964a0c56755cf57b","conditions":"make ui-check passes at the exact head so the committed embed is the reviewed source; the unresolved-ratifier disclosure is pinned by a test that fails when the condition is mutated to false; the queue remains derived from the vocabulary and separate from the commitment population; npx tsc --noEmit is clean and npm test is green at the exact head; architecture, security and simplification conclusions are recorded","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}